The transformation you're describing
Right now the loop is a flat, frictionless faucet:
cut grass → clippings (currency) → spend clippings
What you're proposing turns it into a real simulator loop with pacing, risk, and decisions:
cut grass → fill bag with clippings → (dodge/fight monsters, uncover villagers) → bag fills → run to the Gardener → deposit → gold (+chance of diamonds) → spend gold on tools / bag / swords / climbing gear / villagers → cut better/higher grass faster
That "fill up → bank it" rhythm is the single most important change here. It's what makes every upgrade feel meaningful (bigger bag = fewer trips, faster tool = fills bag quicker, climbing = access to richer roof grass). This is the backbone of games like the mining/pet simulators, and it's a proven, sticky loop. Everything else you listed hangs off it.
First, let's nail the three currencies
Getting the currency roles crystal clear now prevents a lot of pain later:
Resource
What it is
How you get it
What it's for
Clippings
A carried resource, capacity-limited by your bag. No longer a currency.
Cutting grass (richer grass = more per cut)
Deposited at the Gardener to mint gold
Gold
The main currency
Depositing clippings
Tools, bag, swords, climbing gear, villager upgrades — almost everything
Diamonds
Rare premium currency
Chance roll when depositing (and later: monsters, achievements)
A separate premium sink — best tools/bags, exclusive villagers, skins
The nice thing: your grass already has per-type clipReward values (Meadow 3 → Ironweed 80). Those now double as value density — Ironweed fills your bag fast with high-value clippings, so bag capacity becomes a real strategic tension in the richer zones. That's free game design you already built.
One migration note: this flips shop prices from clippings → gold. Since we're still testing, we just wipe saves (bump the store key) when we make the switch — no harm.
Now let's pressure-test each idea
1. The Bag + the Gardener (the foundation)
This is the keystone. Key decisions:
What happens when the bag is full? My recommendation: a hard stop — you can still swing, but full-grass gives 0 clippings and a clear " Bag Full!" banner nudges you to the Gardener. Hard stop makes bag upgrades matter; a soft "overflow is wasted" version is gentler but makes the bag feel pointless. 
Deposit feel: a Gardener NPC with a ProximityPrompt (or a deposit zone, reusing the shop-zone tech we already have). Walk up → hold E → clippings drain into gold with a satisfying count-up + coin sound.
Does the bag survive logout/death? I'd say yes, it persists (it's just a saved number) — losing a full bag to a disconnect feels awful. The risk should come from monsters, not the save system (more below).
2. Diamonds
Design the drop as a roll scaled by deposit size (deposit 500 clippings → more diamond chances than depositing 20), so bigger bags = better diamond odds — another reason to upgrade the bag. Leave the sink flexible for now, but I'd earmark diamonds for a premium upgrade track (top-tier bag/tool, exclusive villagers) so they always feel special. A future "Luck" stat/gamepass is an obvious hook here.
3. Monsters + swords —  the big tonal fork
This is the one decision that changes what kind of game this is. Adding monsters you must fight turns a cozy mowing sim into a light action game. That can be great — but decide the tone deliberately:
Cozy-leaning (my recommendation to start): monsters are optional obstacles. If ignored they just block/roam a patch of grass; your villagers auto-fight them so combat isn't a twitchy burden. Your own sword just makes it faster. Accessible, idle-friendly, fits the vibe.
Risk-leaning: monsters actively attack and can knock clippings out of your unbanked bag — which would create fantastic synergy (it makes "should I bank now or push my luck?" a real decision). But it's punishing and raises the skill floor.
You could even ship cozy first and add the "monsters steal clippings" risk as a later toggle. Either way: monsters should drop loot (gold, occasionally diamonds, maybe rare clippings) so fighting is rewarding, not just a tax.
4. Villagers = a pet/companion system
What you're describing is essentially a pet system, and those are huge retention drivers on Roblox. Reframing it that way unlocks proven patterns:
Found hidden in grass (a discovery/gacha thrill every time you clear a patch).
Rarity tiers (Common → Legendary) with better base stats — gives diamonds and achievements something to gate.
They follow and auto-fight monsters (solves the combat-burden problem in #3).
Upgradeable per-villager (swords, health, speed).
Cap how many are active at once (say 3), so choosing your squad matters and there's a collection meta ("gotta find 'em all").
This one system probably drives more long-term engagement than the combat itself. I'd treat combat and villagers as a pair.
5. Badges / achievements
Two different things worth separating:
In-game achievements (custom): "Cut 10,000 grass," "Deposit 1M clippings," "Defeat 100 monsters" → reward gold/diamonds/skins/buffs. Cheap to build, hooks into events we already fire, and gives diamonds their first meaningful source and skins their first home. High value, low cost — I'd do this early.
Roblox Badges (the real, profile-visible kind via BadgeService): great for prestige milestones ("Reached Ironweed," "First Legendary Villager"). Requires a published game. Nice-to-have layer on top of the custom system.
6. Vertical gameplay — roofs, grass, and climbing
This is the most technically involved, and it intersects directly with the map you're about to build, so let's think about it now even if we build it last:
Roof grass itself is basically free — it's just GrassSurface-tagged parts on your roof geometry. The existing GrassService will coat them automatically.
The climbing tool is the real new tech. Options range from simple (climbable TrussPart "ladders" you place, plus a tool that boosts climb speed) to custom (raycast-based wall-climbing anywhere). I'd start with ladder/truss-based climbing — far simpler and very controllable — with upgrades for climb speed and max climb height/stamina.
Progression gate: climbing gear gating access to high-value roof grass mirrors how tools gate zones — clean, consistent progression.
 Map implication: if you want roof-cutting, build your houses with accessible roof surfaces and a logical spot for ladders from the start. Even if we code climbing later, designing the map for it now saves a rebuild.
How this maps onto what we've already built
Reassuringly, most of this extends our existing structure rather than replacing it:
Currencies/bag → new fields on the saved profile + new stat calculators (bag capacity), and the shop switches to gold. Straightforward.
Gardener → reuses the shop-zone/ProximityPrompt pattern we already have.
Achievements → a config table + a service that listens to events we already fire (grass cut, clippings gained).
Shop → grows into tabbed categories (Tools · Bag · Swords · Climbing · Villagers ·  Premium). The card UI already supports this.
Monsters / villagers / climbing → genuinely new systems (NPC AI, combat, custom controller), the meatiest engineering.
