1. Multiplayer + a hub — how hard is it?
Good news: Roblox is multiplayer by default. Every place you publish is already a client‑server game that multiple people can join — you don't "add networking" the way you would in most engines. Our game is also already written the multiplayer‑safe way: player data is per‑player (PlayerDataService + DataStore), grass cutting is validated server‑side over a RemoteEvent, and the HUD is built per‑client. So the engine-level multiplayer is essentially done.
The real work is design, and it comes down to one question — who shares the world?
Shared world (easy): everyone in a server mows the same neighborhood together. Simple, but your teddy‑bear story is personal, so it gets weird if 10 strangers are in "your" transformed neighborhood.
Per‑player / per‑group instanced world (moderate): each player (or each friend group) gets their own copy of the neighborhood. This fits the story much better.
The hub → game flow you described maps to two standard patterns:
One place, zones inside it (easiest). The hub is just an area in the same place; you move the player's character (or teleport them within the place) when they "start." Lowest complexity, but everyone's still on one shared server.
Multiple places + TeleportService (the real "hub" pattern, moderate). The hub is one place; "the actual game" is a second place in the same universe. When a player (or a party of friends) starts, you call TeleportService:TeleportAsync() to send them into a reserved server — a private instance just for them/their group. This is exactly how most lobby‑then‑round games work, and it's what gives each player their own story instance while still allowing co‑op.
Difficulty verdict:
Making the current game multiplayer‑correct: already ~90% there.
A hub as a zone in the same place: easy.
A proper hub with TeleportService + reserved servers + a little party/matchmaking: moderate — the fiddly parts are publishing a multi‑place universe, passing teleport data, and handling teleport failures gracefully.
My recommendation: build the cutscene + gameplay first as a single place, and add the hub/teleport layer as its own phase once the core loop feels good. It slots in cleanly later.
2. How cutscenes & camera control work in Roblox
Cutscenes are just camera manipulation, and the camera is client‑side — each player has their own workspace.CurrentCamera. So a cutscene is a LocalScript (a Controller, in our project's structure) that runs when the player joins/spawns.
The core moves:
local cam = workspace.CurrentCamera

-- 1. Take control away from the player
cam.CameraType = Enum.CameraType.Scriptable

-- 2. Point it wherever you want (position, look-at target)
cam.CFrame = CFrame.new(startPosition, targetPosition)

-- 3. Smoothly move between shots with TweenService
local TweenService = game:GetService("TweenService")
local info = TweenInfo.new(3, Enum.EasingStyle.Sine, Enum.EasingDirection.InOut) -- 3 seconds
local shot = TweenService:Create(cam, info, { CFrame = nextShotCFrame })
shot:Play()
shot.Completed:Wait() -- then play the next shot

-- 4. When the cutscene ends, hand control back
cam.CameraType = Enum.CameraType.Custom
​
For your specific scene, the sequence is basically: freeze the player (so they can't walk during it), play a series of camera shots (bedroom → teddy tips off the windowsill → camera follows it down → pull back to reveal the transformed plains), optionally animate a few props (the teddy falling is just a CFrame tween on the teddy model), then release the camera + player into gameplay. I'd also add a "Skip" button so returning players aren't forced to rewatch it.
Two ways to author the actual shots:
Camera‑marker Parts + tweens (recommended for you). You place invisible Parts in Studio where you want each camera shot, and the script tweens between them in order. Beginner‑friendly because you position cameras visually in Studio instead of guessing numbers.
Moon Animator 2 (advanced, optional). A timeline animation plugin that can animate the camera and characters like real animation software. More powerful, but playing its animations back in a live game is more involved. I'd skip it for now.
