Those three facts explain both bugs exactly:
The falling torso — it was an unanchored part attached to literally nothing, so gravity took it. Adding a root joint stopped that correctly.
"Only his chest moves" — animations address joints by name. A stock R6 animation drives RootJoint/Neck/Right Shoulder/Left Shoulder/Right Hip/Left Hip. This rig has none of those names, so a stock animation could move nothing at all — until my fix added a Motor6D named RootJoint, which handed the animation the one name it recognized. I accidentally created the second bug while fixing the first.
The fix is the opposite of what you'd guess
I did not rename the joints to the standard set, because the asset ships its own animations — Animations/Idle (15455578967) and Animations/Attack (15454224032) — authored against these custom joint names. Renaming would break them. Instead:
The Torso is now held by a Weld, not a Motor6D. Welds carry no animation channel, so no animation track can ever grab the torso and make it the only moving part. The old failure mode is now structurally impossible, not just avoided.
PlayIdle prefers the rig's own bundled idle, falling back to the stock catalog idle only for rigs whose joints are standard-named. Since that bundled idle was authored for this exact skeleton, the whole body animates.
Rigs already wired correctly are untouched — Sprigman is built in code with proper joint names, so he keeps the stock idle and is unaffected.
