Mow the Kingdom — Complete Context Brief
A self-contained description of the game, its systems, and its codebase. Written to be pasted to another AI as context.
1. What the game is
Mow the Kingdom is a Roblox incremental/simulator game built in Luau. The player mows grass to earn currency, spends it on better tools and upgrades, and pushes outward into progressively tougher terrain. It is single-player-focused with optional co-op.
Premise: A kid is at their desk when their teddy bear falls out the bedroom window. They go outside to retrieve it — and find their suburban neighborhood has been overtaken by an endless plains biome. The teddy bear lands out in the grass. Reclaiming the land, one tile at a time, is the game.
The intro is a scripted cinematic cutscene (R6 rig, ~6 custom animations) showing the kid typing, the bear falling, and the run outside. The neighborhood is then swapped for the plains behind a black fade, so the world transformation is felt rather than explained.
Tone/aesthetic: bright, chunky, low-poly. Originally conceived as medieval-fantasy ("Kingdom of Verdania"); the current shipped fiction is the suburban-neighborhood-turned-plains version. Some legacy naming (the title itself, "Kingdom" as the world folder name) survives from the older concept.
2. The core gameplay loop
The 30-second loop the whole game is tuned around:
Mow grass. Equip a tool, hold left-click, sweep across grass. Each cut removes a slab and yields clippings.
Fill your backpack. Clippings are carried, not banked. The backpack has a hard capacity cap — when full, mowing stops paying and the player is nudged to bank.
Bank at the Gardener. Walk into the Gardener's zone to convert clippings → gold, with a chance at diamonds.
Spend at the Shop. Buy tools, backpacks, upgrades, auto-mowers.
Push outward. Better gear survives tougher grass in the next ring, which pays more.
Planned extensions to the loop (designed, not yet built): monsters guard the outer rings and steal unbanked clippings, so the risk of carrying a full bag rises the further out you go; swords let you fight back; defeating monsters yields per-zone essence, which feeds pets/villagers and quests.
