Done — all four new issues are fixed, plus the cutscene backpack from before (you uploaded the files for it). Here's what changed:
1. Lock-on camera tilt (now angles ~25° down, never up)
The old camera aimed at a point above the player and, since it sat below the enemy's torso, it tilted up — worse as the enemy got closer. Rewrote the framing in TargetingController: the camera is now raised so the view onto the enemy is a fixed 25° downward tilt (CAM_PITCH). It stays centered on the enemy and never pitches up.
To stop the camera "flying up" for far targets, the full 25° applies within PITCH_REF_DIST (18 studs); beyond that it eases off gently. Zoom still slides the camera along that angle, keeping the 4/2 shoulder framing.
2. Blade turning neon white permanently
Root cause: the hit-flash captured the blade's current color/material as the "original" every hit. A second kill within the 0.12s flash window captured the flashed white/Neon as the original, then restored to that forever. Fixed with a flash token: the true base look is snapshotted only at the start of a burst, and only the last flash's restore runs — so it always returns to the real blade color.
3. Start with the first sword
Added ensureStarterSword in SwordService: every player now owns + equips Meadow Cutlass by default (level 1), exactly like the free starter shears, so they're armed the moment grass-cutting (and monster spawns) begin.
4. Crosshair red-but-can't-hit margin
The crosshair used a loose distance check (reach + 3 studs) with no direction, so it went red past actual hit range and when strafing around a target. Rewrote CombatRange with IsInSwingBox, which mirrors the server's real swing box: in front, within reach, and within the box width (IN_RANGE_PAD 3→0.5, LATERAL_HALF 2.5). Both shift-lock and lock-on crosshairs now use it, so red = a swing will actually connect.
Bonus — cutscene backpack (from last time): The pack is now deferred during the intro (mirroring how tools are held back) and put on the moment the cutscene finishes, so it's no longer visible on the player's back mid-scene.
